"""P4: the ADR withdraws the figures the tool got wrong, and says so where they were.

`ADR-0057` is the decision that the worker count follows what the machine can
hold, and its own decision 3 says the numbers are measured by a tool rather
than asserted in a comment. `tools/measure_build_memory.py` loaded the national
timetable as a statement with no assignment, so CPython freed it before the
sample was taken and the sample read the arenas Python had not returned to the
operating system. The 325 MB initialiser and the 852 MB worker came out of that,
and they are wrong by six times in the reassuring direction.

A controlled A/B on the real feed: holding the reference reads 1,918 MB private,
dropping it reads 316 MB. Through the pool's own `init_worker` and
`build_page_job`, one worker is 2,100 MB after initialising and 2,188 MB
assembling pages, against a `WORKER_BYTES` of 900 MB.

So this pass withdraws the two figures where they are stated -- struck through
in the table rather than deleted, because a withdrawn number that has left no
trace is a number someone will measure again -- and adds the correction beneath
it, with the A/B that produced it. Decision 3 gains the rule the bug teaches:
**a measurement must hold what it measures.** The two comments that quoted the
withdrawn figures as though they justified their constants are corrected to say
what is true of them now, which is that one is sound and the other is not.

`WORKER_BYTES` is deliberately unchanged. The corrected worker figures say the
four-worker budget does not hold; the recorded four-worker tree in the same ADR
says four workers fitted in 2.9 GB with the parent down to 120 MB, which cannot
be true at 2.1 GB each. One of those is now known to be a measurement error,
the other is unexplained, and changing a constant that decides the worker count
on every machine and in CI on the strength of a contradiction is not a decision a
comment can make. Settling it needs one real four-worker build, which this
machine does not have the memory for; the correction says so, and the two tools
that will settle it -- `tools/measure_build_memory.py`, which now holds its
subject, and `tools/measure_worker_pages.py`, which measures one real worker
without needing four -- are named under "Read by running".

Generated by _cache/make_p4.py, which reads every anchor out of git and off
disk, so an anchor cannot drift from the text it is meant to match.
"""

